Date: Mon, 28 Jun 93 11:03:10 -0700
From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Flying snakes

I think the sequence was from one of the video series of that ubiquitous
arboreal primate, David Attenborough.  If memory serves, the animal's
popular name is the paradise tree snake of Borneo.  It doesn't so much
"swim" as use wriggling motions of its body to steer a little bit -- its
glide angle is not terribly impressive.

It is interesting that at the level of a few layers of hearsay and a
couple of drinks removed, the actions of this critter resemble those
attributed to the Chinese imperial dragon, a wingless, limbless serpent
that flew by casting its body onto the wind and undulating its coils.
Borneo is sufficiently close to China that the chinese might have known
about the paradise tree snake in ancient times; I wonder if it is the
origin of the myth?

Flight seems to be an extremely useful adaptation.  Full flight has
been evolved something like half a dozen times in the known history of
the animal kingdom (twice for insects, I think, plus birds, bats,
pteranodons and pterosaurs), and the ability to glide -- which includes
"the flying squirrel trick" -- has cropped up in frogs, snakes, lizards,
fish (the flying fish, of course) ("Barracuda closing fast on the
starboard quarter, Cap'n!"  "Just a few more knots and we'll be ready
for the jump to lightspeed -- er, airspeed ...") several times in
rodents, in squirrel-like marsupials, and no doubt elsewhere.

                                   -- Jay Freeman

