Date: Mon, 28 Jun 93 11:17:25 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

I've been trying to put my finger on an important notion that does not seem
to yet be adequately covered in the AIT-logical depth literature--the
concept of how "important" or "critical" a bit is.

To quickly get to the point:

- a gas is often cited as a maximally complex (or close to maximally
complex) system, with zillions of bits in the zillions of gas molecule
positions and momenta...but the "properties" of a gas, that we see (a
caveat I'll come back to), are not changed by the twiddling of any of these
bits. Thus, lots of bits, but they're nearly all "unimportant."

(Closely related to this is the idea of "structure," or of fitness for some
function. A gas has no structure, essentially, and so its configuration of
particle positions and velocities is relatively unimportant--except for a
tiny fraction of special states. Any "mutation" (bit twiddling) is likely
to have no effect on the "fitness" fo the gas as a system. The same cannot
be said, of, say, the plans for the Pentium microprocessor. It has fewer
bits than a cc of gas has, but the bits are vastly more important and the
"fitness" of the system is greatly altered by mutations--probably improved.
:-} )

- a crystal has relatively low complexity (by whatever measure), and yet
the change of a single atom can create a flaw, a dislocation, which greatly
affects the integrity or "fitness" (in a strange sense) of the crystal.
Thus, few bits, but great sensitivity to bit changes.

(A crystal has relatively few bits but a lot of "structure.")

- Derek Zahn used to argue that a "blended-up" frog has more "complexity"
than an unblended frog. (His posting below seems to change positions on
this.) But altering the bits in the blended-up frog makes little difference
(in function/fitness), whereas changing the bits in an unblended frog
(especially if we're talking about the DNA in the frog's germ cells, which
is a more relevant example) makes a potentially greater difference for the
fitness of the resultant frog. Thus, the unblended frog has more "critical"
bits than the blended frog has.

(Structure again.)

- a poker hand is often used to illustrate outcomes, "important" outcomes
(like a royal flush), and the basics of probability. It is true that any
specific hand is as likely as one of the royal flushes (10-J-Q-K-A in one
suit), but "nearly all" poker hands are "less interesting" and are not much
changed by twiddling one of the cards.

(If fitness is determined by the standard values of poker, then whether one
"draws" (twiddles bits, as it were) depends on what one already has.
Holding a flush or a straight is "maximally complex" and no organism/player
intent on winning/surviving would draw to it. Thus, some hands in poker
have more "critical bits" than "blended" hands. (Sorry for all the
quotation marks, but these terms are of course very slippery.)

I'm hoping to better formalize this notion of structure and critical bits.


Anyway, Derek Zahn wrote:

>Yikes!  Busy weekend for the seminar!

Yes, I think we're getting an interesting handle on these issues. I just
sent Greg Chaitin some e-mail mentioning our stuff on Go, space-time
tradeoffs, fitness functions, etc. This was also to tell him about our
"virtual seminar."

