>but widely) is that were the entire game tree to be searched with
>minimax, it would turn out that the game is a draw under optimal
>strategy.  If this is true, then how are we to define the fitness
>"landscape" of chess programs?  One way would be to compare its
>performance to that of (any) optimal strategy.  But to me this
>seems unreasonable because the program will never be asked to play
>against the optimal strategy.  A better measure is probably how well

Yes, I fully agree of course that the talk of "optimal strategies" breaks
down completely with even fairly simple games (though I think people should
study optimal strategies....sometimes they can be found through purely
formal means, even for complex games) and that "agents of bounded
rationality" are a better fit to the real world, especially as the agents
get more powerful!

(This may sound paradoxical...it's not. As agents get more powerful, so do
their opponents. Just like the real market, the real economy. The thoughts
of other players, of their complex aspirations, goals, etc., become harder
to anticipate. This bears directly on issues of predicting economic
outcomes.)

....stuff elided to conserve some space....

>On Tim's program size/running time graph, the set of "good" go players
>form a curve that asymptotes to each axis in the limit -- simple tree
>search given humongous computing resources, and simple table lookup
>given humongous program size.

......good points elided.....

>  * The structure of the curve in the tradeoff region is quite important.
>  * Evolution is limited in what parts of the space it will search.
>    It may be that a nanotech frog will be a superior sort of frog
>    and have less "complexity" -- but natural processes of evolution
>    (biological or perhaps even industrial) won't find it.

Yes on both of these points. This space-time picture (and there are other
axes, perhaps something along the lines of "critical bits" that I mentioned
above) has "structure" for various systems....stable regions, crevasses,
mountain ranges, etc. (I won't say what or where they are...I just suspect
that this space has various topologies and metrics on it, loosely
speaking.)


>A further note on complexity, implied by the above.  I'm starting
>to believe that maybe the "classical" AIT view of complexity may
>be the appropriate one for living things after all.  In the past
>I've argued that the incompressibility version of complexity doesn't
>work because it would imply a the post-blender frog is more complex
>that the pre-blender frog -- in conflict with intuition.  However,
>if we can somehow attach the stipulation that the object under study
>exists on a complex fitness landscape (synonymous with the above
>"complex" "functionality") with high fitness, the post-blender frog
>has high complexity as fertilizer, but isn't much of a frog!

Yes, this is the key. (I could gloat here, now that people are coming
around to my overall view, but I won't, as I don't have but a tiny fraction
of the insights I hope I'll eventually have.)

The blended-up frog does *not* have more complexity, in a sense of
"critical" bits for some fitness landscape, any more than a scrambled
program has more information content than an unscrambled program.

Now, if we came across a hideously complex-looking program, with
hacker-like tricks (reentrant code, lots of subroutine calls and jumps,
undocumented and apparently chaotic-looking routines, etc.), and we knew
this program "did something" like build an organism or balance the federal
budget, then we might be justified in saying it did have real complexity.

But not a randomized program, which almost certainly "halts" the machine it
is intended to run on. (This may provide an avenue for looking at
"criticality"...restricting ourselves to "running" programs.)

I suspect real life, real organisms, can be characterized as having fairly
high complexity, and also having a high fraction of critical bits. 

Let's combine both space and time complexity into the x-axis, and put
"criticality" or "structure" on the y-axis:


      ^             ^
      |             |  
      |             |  A                       A = small computer program,
                    |                            but all bits are important
                    |C                         C = crystal..few bits, most
 "criticality"      |                            are important
                    |
   structure        |           B              B = frog DNA...megabytes, not
                    |                            all matter very much
                    |
                    |
 (May's notions)    |
                    |                    D     D = a cc of gas...lots of bits,
                    |_ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ >      almost none of them matter
                                
                          program size--->
                     
                        (space and time, combined measure)   

                        (Chaitin, Bennett, Wolfram, etc.)      
              
  
It may seem odd that frogs and other life lie somewhere in the middle (this
is a log scale, so it's not really in the "middle"), but most organisms can
tolerate a fair number of mutations...the "quasi-species" idea of Manfred
Eigen (see the latest "Scientific American"). Life is complex, but is also
more fault-tolerant and less fragile than, say, current-generation computer
programs. I'll say more about this later.                   
  
                          
------

(These issues are deep indeed and bear on issues of entropy and
reversibility, Maxwell's demon, phase spaces, etc. "Strings as gases" is a
tie-in. Structure is the same as critical bits. More work is needed.)


-Tim, who is heartened by the progress we're apparently making

