Michael Clive Price writes:
>
>Nearly 2 months ago Paul Cisek said:
>
>> However, there is increasing evidence and opinion that subcortical
>> structures are the actual substrate for much of what we call cognition
>> and that the cerebrum and cerebellum "merely" provide support, or side
>> paths.  In human development (ontogeny follows phylogeny!) it appears
>> that the cerebral cortex takes over in adults some of the
>> functionality implemented in infants by the subcortical structures.
>
>I'd like to hear more about this...

Try this article:
Stelzner, D. "Ontogeny of the encephalization process" in W. Greenough & J.
Juraska (eds) _Developmental_Neuropsychobiology_, Academic press, Orlando
(pp. 241-270).
I can perhaps dig up more, this was the first that fell into my hand...

>...Does this imply that many of our
>infantile experiences are stored at the subcortical level and become
>inaccessible to our consciousness as it shifts to the higher cortical
>location?  Does this explain the difficulty in recalling early childhood
>memories?  Where can I find out more?

Inaccessible to consciousness?  I'm not sure what that means...  ;-)

You seem to assume that experiences are stored in some explicit way (as images,
fragments of sound...) in the immature brain, and then ask where this might
happen.  I would rather say that the extent of functional changes that you've
undergone since childhood prevents the reproduction of behavior that implies
memory when you are placed in a recall inducing situation (this is not just
a play on words).

As far as where memory is stored - lots of people say the hippocampus or the
cerebellum, but that's only because we've found long-term activity-dependent
changes there, more likely it happens all over the place.

As to where you can find out more, I don't know.  Nobody understands memory,
much less the awareness of it.  Current theories are scattered among many
fields, but I don't think any would be able to answer your particular
question.

>
>What is the dominant brain structure in dinosaurs?  Did they have
>cortexes?
>

I don't know.  However, many animals (like fish) do not possess either the
cerebral or cerebellar cortices.  If you admit that these animals are capable
of associative learning, and also believe that in humans such association is
done by the cortex, then you have to conclude that these functions either
migrate from subcortical to cortical substrates or are augmented by the cortex
as evolution proceeds.  If you then take the `ontogeny follows phylogeny'
argument, the migration of functions during development does not seem so
far-fetched.

-Paul

